## 4. Audio timestamps on answers (`a34d8a8`)

Answers now cite where in the recording the supporting context came from:

```
Ask about the audio (or 'exit' to quit): what is the main topic being discussed?

Based on the context spoken at 1:51:28-1:51:50, the main topic is ...

  [audio 1:51:28-1:51:50]
```

The timestamp is also given to Claude alongside the context, so "when was X
mentioned?" can be answered directly.

- **`chunk_segments()`** (`ChunckAndEmbed.py`) chunks timestamped segments rather
  than raw text. A Whisper segment can span 30 seconds and several sentences, which
  is too coarse to cite, so sentence times are interpolated across the segment by
  character position.
- **Transcripts cached as JSON** (`transcripts/<name>.json`), since plain text cannot
  carry timings. A `.txt` copy is written alongside for reading. A plain-text
  transcript passed via `--transcript` still works, just without citations.
- **`format_clock()`** (`transcribe.py`) is the single place a time is rendered for
  humans, switching to `H:MM:SS` once the audio passes an hour.

Precision trade-offs: `--batch-size` is much faster but yields ~30 s segments;
`--whisper-model large-v3` segments more accurately than `base`.

### Repository hygiene

`__pycache__` was tracked from an earlier commit and rewritten on every run.
`.gitignore` already covered it; the files needed `git rm --cached`. `.gitignore`
also gained `transcripts/` and `__pycache__/`.

---

## 5. Embedding batching (`a34d8a8`, corrected in `67c0225`)

Voyage's free tier allows 3 requests and 10K tokens per minute. A 127-minute
transcript is ~32K tokens and was rejected outright as a single request, so indexing
long audio failed with `voyageai.error.RateLimitError`.

`generate_embedding()` now splits the work into token-budgeted requests and retries
with backoff. Retrying rather than sleeping pre-emptively keeps paid accounts running
at full speed.

`67c0225` then closed three gaps that only stayed hidden because this pipeline feeds
the function short English chunks:

1. **Real tokenizer instead of `len//4`.** The heuristic ran ~12% over on English
   prose but ~45% **under** on CJK and code — three sample texts counted
   160/340/101 real tokens against guesses of 90/190/105. Underestimating is the
   direction that gets a request rejected. Even the English test transcript came to
   32,182 real tokens against a ~27,000 estimate. Counting is local and cheap once
   the tokenizer is cached (369 chunks in under 0.1 s), and falls back to the
   heuristic when it cannot be loaded, so an offline run still works.
2. **A text larger than the whole budget is sent on its own, with a warning.** It was
   already ending up alone as a side effect of the windowing, but silently, so an
   over-long chunk being truncated by Voyage gave no clue why.
3. **The budget is a keyword argument**, not a module constant pinned to the free
   tier. The 32K-token transcript goes out as one request at
   `max_tokens_per_request=40000` instead of five at the default.

Also retries `ServerError`, `ServiceUnavailableError` and `APIConnectionError`, not
just `RateLimitError` — a dropped connection previously failed a whole indexing run.
Backoff starts at 20 s for rate limits (measured per minute) and 2 s for everything
else. `AuthenticationError` and malformed requests still fail immediately, because
waiting cannot help.

---

## 6. `--transcript` path resolution (`507b74c`)

`--transcript wrd.json` failed with "transcript not found" even though the file sat
in `transcripts/wrd.json`, because the value was treated as a literal path — and that
directory is where the script itself writes, so it is the likeliest thing to be passed
by name.

`resolve_transcript()` tries the literal path, then the same name inside
`transcripts/`, then both with `.json` and `.txt` appended when no suffix was given.
A bare name prefers `.json`, the form carrying segment timings. All of these now
resolve to `transcripts/wrd.json`:

```
wrd    wrd.json    transcripts/wrd.json    transcripts\wrd.json
```

`Learn_Transformer` in the repo root still resolves as before. When nothing matches,
the error lists what is cached and marks which entries are timestamped.

